Question 18: Who is Buying the Tickets?

AIM: Compare internal vs external visitors with stacked and grouped bar charts.

Code:
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np

day = ['Day 1','Day 2','Day 3']
internal = [120,150,90]
external = [30,80,160]

plt.bar(day, internal, label='Internal')
plt.bar(day, external, bottom=internal, label='External')
plt.title("Tickets Sold (Stacked)")
plt.legend()
plt.show()

x = np.arange(len(day))
plt.bar(x-0.2, internal, width=0.4, label='Internal')
plt.bar(x+0.2, external, width=0.4, label='External')
plt.xticks(x, day)
plt.title("Tickets Sold (Grouped)")
plt.legend()
plt.show()

totals = np.array(internal) + np.array(external)
shares = np.array(external)/totals*100
print("Totals:", totals)
print("External shares:", shares.round(1))


Output:
Totals: [150 230 250]
External shares: [20.0 34.8 64.0]

Explanation:  
Functions used: bar(), legend(), title(), xticks()
Usage: bar() to draw bars, legend() to label groups, title() to add chart titles, xticks() to set day labels
Interpretation: Stacked bars show total footfall better, grouped bars compare internal vs external more clearly. 
External share rises from 20% to 64%, with Day 3 most dependent on outside visitors
